In grade(), after computing the IDs, compare counts and surface a non-fatal warning. Minimal change in WorkbookGradingView.swift around lines 591-604:

let (wbIDs, ansIDs) = await Task.detached {
    (WorkbookImageStore.saveAll(wbImages), WorkbookImageStore.saveAll(ansImages))
}.value
await MainActor.run {
    let session = GradedSession(...)  // unchanged
    WorkbookGradingHistoryStore.shared.add(session)
    if wbIDs.count != wbImages.count || ansIDs.count != ansImages.count {
        let lost = (wbImages.count - wbIDs.count) + (ansImages.count - ansIDs.count)
        errorMessage = String(localized: "Graded successfully, but \(lost) scan(s) could not be saved to history.")
    }
    ...
}

This keeps the grading result intact while informing the user that the saved history entry is incomplete. Optionally, make WorkbookImageStore.save throwing so callers can react per-image, but the count comparison is the minimal fix.

Make the label/state content-derived so it reflects actual bank membership instead of a one-shot local flag. Replace the `@State private var addedAll = false` + the button body with bank-membership-driven logic:

In `GradedSessionDetailView`, compute membership rather than tracking it:

  private var allInBank: Bool {
      !session.questions.isEmpty && session.questions.allSatisfy { bank.contains($0) }
  }

Then in `addToBankButton`:

  Button {
      for question in session.questions { _ = bank.add(question) }
  } label: {
      Label(allInBank ? "已加入题库 · Added to Bank" : "全部加入题库 · Add all to Review Bank",
            systemImage: allInBank ? "tray.full.fill" : "tray.and.arrow.down.fill")
          .fitSingleLine()
          .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)
  }
  .buttonStyle(.bordered)
  .disabled(allInBank)

Because `bank` is `@Observable`, `allInBank` re-evaluates after `add()` mutates `questions`, so the label updates and the button disables only once everything is truly present — and on first open it correctly shows "Added to Bank" if all questions were already banked from the live screen. The `addedAll` @State can be removed. Optionally, count actual insertions (`add()` returns Bool) to surface "Added N" feedback, but the membership-based approach alone resolves both the stale-label and first-open-inconsistency issues and matches the live screen's behavior.

Make the post-grade save idempotent for the current input set. Minimal fix: gate the persistence block on didSaveSession (which already exists and is reset only at the start of grade()), and re-evaluate it through canGrade so the button disables after a successful run.

Concretely, in grade()'s success MainActor.run block (lines 597-610), wrap the three persistence calls so they only run when not already saved for this set:

    if !didSaveSession {
        let session = GradedSession(... )
        WorkbookGradingHistoryStore.shared.add(session)
        if !graded.questions.isEmpty {
            LearningActivityStore.shared.recordQuestionsGraded(graded.questions.count)
        }
    }
    didSaveSession = true
    result = graded
    isGrading = false

and also skip the duplicate disk write by only calling WorkbookImageStore.saveAll(...) when !didSaveSession (move the detached saveAll into the guarded path, or check the flag before line 591-593).

Then add didSaveSession (false) to canGrade so the Grade button disables once a set has been graded, until the user changes the inputs:

    private var canGrade: Bool {
        (!workbookImages.isEmpty || !answerImages.isEmpty) && gradingProvider != nil && !isGrading && !didSaveSession
    }

To allow legitimate re-grading after edits, reset didSaveSession=false in the image add/remove/clear handlers (addWorkbookPhotos, addAnswerPhotos, file/drop handlers, onRemove, onClear, addCapturedImage). Simplest alternative if re-grade is never intended: clear workbookImages/answerImages after a successful grade so canGrade naturally returns false.

Make the persistence side effects in grade() run once per distinct input set. Minimal approach: gate the persistence block (lines 597-609) and clear the gate whenever inputs change.

Add state:
  @State private var lastGradedSignature: Int?

Compute a signature from the snapshotted inputs after gradeWorkbook returns, e.g.:
  var hasher = Hasher()
  hasher.combine(wbImages); hasher.combine(ansImages); hasher.combine(custom)
  let signature = hasher.finalize()

Then inside the MainActor.run success block, only persist when the signature differs:
  if signature != lastGradedSignature {
      let session = GradedSession(...)
      WorkbookGradingHistoryStore.shared.add(session)
      if !graded.questions.isEmpty {
          LearningActivityStore.shared.recordQuestionsGraded(graded.questions.count)
      }
      lastGradedSignature = signature
      didSaveSession = true
  }
  result = graded
  isGrading = false

Invalidate lastGradedSignature whenever images or customInstructions change (in the onRemove/onClear/onDrop/add* handlers, or via .onChange of workbookImages/answerImages/customInstructions set lastGradedSignature = nil) so an edited-then-regraded run does persist exactly once.

Simpler alternative: after a successful grade, reset the inputs (workbookItems = []; workbookImages = []; answerItems = []; answerImages = []) so canGrade becomes false until the user adds new photos — at the cost of losing the on-screen thumbnails. The signature approach is preferred because it preserves the displayed inputs while still preventing double-counting.

If hardening is still desired (defensive only, not a live-bug fix), re-derive the authoritative key after load in LearningActivityStore.loadActivities(), since the dictionary key is the source of truth:

private func loadActivities() {
    if let decoded = PersistentCodableStore.load([String: DailyActivity].self, key: userDefaultsKey) {
        // The map key is authoritative; repair any decoded dateKey field that
        // drifted from it (e.g. tolerant-decode fallback on a corrupt payload).
        activities = decoded.reduce(into: [:]) { result, pair in
            let (mapKey, activity) = pair
            if activity.dateKey == mapKey {
                result[mapKey] = activity
            } else {
                result[mapKey] = DailyActivity(
                    dateKey: mapKey,
                    wordsLearned: activity.wordsLearned,
                    reviewsCompleted: activity.reviewsCompleted,
                    translationsMade: activity.translationsMade,
                    questionsGraded: activity.questionsGraded,
                    wordsByPartOfSpeech: activity.wordsByPartOfSpeech
                )
            }
        }
    }
    longestStreak = UserDefaults.standard.integer(forKey: "longestStreak")
}

This guarantees the field can never disagree with its map key. Alternatively, the line-82 fallback could decode into a sentinel empty string, but that yields a "today" date via `date`'s `?? Date()` path anyway, so repairing from the map key is the cleaner option.

